Avalanche Kernel 0.4.0 source, user applications, avalanche.elf and the
Avalanche filesystem image are covered by ../LICENSE (MIT).

avalanche.iso additionally contains unmodified GNU GRUB 2.12-9+deb13u2
binaries from Debian, distributed under the respective licenses in
GRUB-COPYRIGHT. Corresponding upstream source and Debian patches/build
rules are included in grub-source/. GRUB is a separate bootloader, not
part of the Avalanche kernel. The MIT license does not relicense GRUB.

Implemented: a 256-slot ring-3 process table, preemptive priority scheduling
with aging and time slices, per-process page tables and kernel stacks, background
jobs, sleep/input/child waits, process termination, and x87/FXSAVE SSE
context preservation. Native applications include the userspace shell,
hello, cat, UDP, HTTP and a CPU-bound worker.

Fork uses reference-counted copy-on-write pages. Anonymous mappings are
allocated on demand. The graphical Magenta compositor supports rounded,
translucent windows, blurred panels, shadows, animations and TrueType text.
The bundled DejaVu font retains its license in ../assets/FONT-LICENSE.
The graphical desktop uses Nuklear and its Catppuccin themes under the
MIT licenses in ../vendor/nuklear/. Source revisions are in ORIGIN.md.
Seven applications provide a native shell terminal, directory browser,
text editor, process manager, network tools, package manager and settings.
Appearance preferences persist on the writable disk. The ISO includes
the toolkit, palette and font license notices in /licenses.

The ext4 root backend supports 4096-byte blocks, extent trees, 64-bit
filesystem descriptors, flex_bg layouts, sparse file growth beyond 4 GiB,
file creation, replacement and removal. Metadata changes and data writes
use bounded multi-block JBD2 transactions and committed-transaction replay.
The package manager stores its binaries and versioned SHA-256 manifests
on ext4, resolves catalog dependencies, and downloads HTTP packages through
the kernel network stack. The block drivers use a writeback cache.

Linux compatibility is a specific i386 static-ELF/int-0x80 ABI subset:
ELF initial argc/argv/envp/auxv, fork, execve, exit, waitpid, PID/UID calls,
file descriptors with shared fork offsets, open/read/write/close, dup,
lseek/_llseek, fcntl, unlink, stat/fstat/stat64/fstat64, getdents64,
brk, anonymous mmap/mmap2/munmap/mprotect, set_thread_area TLS,
set_tid_address, nanosleep, monotonic clocks, uname, terminal queries,
and sched_yield. The linuxhello and linuxfork executables use Linux
syscall numbers directly, without the Avalanche SDK. Unimplemented calls
return -ENOSYS.

Hardware: PCI, legacy ATA PIO/LBA28, AHCI SATA DMA48, legacy/transitional
VirtIO block and network, RTL8139, E1000, PIC/PIT, CMOS RTC, COM1 serial,
PS/2 keyboard/mouse, PC speaker and RGB framebuffer. VirtIO uses the
legacy PCI I/O interface and supports queue sizes through 1024; modern
VirtIO-only/MMIO devices are not supported. AHCI uses the first discovered
controller and at most eight SATA ports. Block I/O requires 512-byte
logical sectors. No native USB, NVMe, Wi-Fi, audio mixer or accelerated GPU.

Networking: ARP, IPv4, ICMP, UDP, DHCP, DNS, an active-open TCP stream,
and HTTP/1.0 clients. TCP has one process-owned connection, in-order
receive, stop-and-wait sending and bounded retries; no listen API, SACK,
window scaling, congestion control or TIME_WAIT table. No TLS, IPv6 or
IP fragmentation/reassembly. DHCP renewal is manual. Drivers are polled;
kernel syscalls are serialized, while user code is timer-preempted.

Filesystem: checksummed dual metadata banks and copy-on-write file slots,
a flat namespace with 32 files of at most 64 KiB each, in its own MBR type
0x7f partition. Without a recognized disk, files use volatile RAM. The
supplied data image contains nine application executables. The physical
allocator manages usable RAM below 128 MiB; user memory is bounded to
8 MiB per process and twelve page tables. The CPU must support x86-64 long mode.
The kernel and native applications are 64-bit; the UEFI loader targets x86-64 firmware.
Native ELF64 uses low-4-GiB user addresses and the existing bounded service
ABI. Linux ELF32 uses compatibility mode.
